@page "/blazor-rules"
<PageTitle>Blazor-aware rules · Blazor Accessibility Toolkit</PageTitle>

<div class="page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Component tests · bUnit</p>
    <h1>Rules only a Blazor-aware tool can see</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        A browser scanner sees the DOM. Some of the most common Blazor accessibility bugs are not visible there: event
        handlers live in the .NET render tree, and others come from patterns in Blazor's own components and templates.
        The toolkit adds rules for them. All four pairs below are checked without a browser.
    </p>
</div>

<RunAllTests />

<div class="callout">
    <p>
        Most of these rules report <strong>potential issues</strong>: likely problems that need a person to confirm.
        <code>ShouldHaveNoViolations()</code> lists them without failing, and
        <code>ShouldHaveNoViolations(AccessibilityExpectation.Strict)</code> fails on them, as these tests do.
        The CI gate makes the same distinction (<code>--fail-on-potential</code>).
    </p>
</div>

<section aria-labelledby="click-handlers">
    <h2 id="click-handlers">Click handlers on elements that are not interactive</h2>
    <p>
        Rule <code>blazor-click-handler-semantics</code>. A <code>&lt;div @@onclick&gt;</code> works with a mouse, but
        keyboard users cannot reach or activate it, and screen readers do not know it does anything. Browsers cannot see
        Blazor's handler, so axe-core reports nothing. bUnit's render tree shows it.
    </p>
    <ExampleCard Title="A clickable card" Defect="true" Example="BlazorRuleExamples.ClickableCardAudit" Sources="@(["ClickableCard.razor"])"
                 Hears="Shipping, heading, level 3" HearsLabel="The card is announced as" Affects="@(["Keyboard users", "Screen reader users", "Switch users"])">
        <ChildContent><p>Click the card to open it. Then try with the keyboard: Tab skips it.</p></ChildContent>
        <Stage><ClickableCard Title="Shipping" Details="Ships in 2 days." /></Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
    <ExampleCard Title="A disclosure button" Example="BlazorRuleExamples.DisclosureCardAudit" Sources="@(["DisclosureCard.razor"])"
                 Hears="Shipping, button, collapsed">
        <Stage><DisclosureCard Title="Shipping" Details="Ships in 2 days." /></Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="validation">
    <h2 id="validation">Validation messages that are not connected to their fields</h2>
    <p>
        Rules <code>blazor-validation-message-association</code> and <code>blazor-invalid-without-error-reference</code>.
        With the pattern from the Blazor templates, <code>InputText</code> marks the field <code>aria-invalid="true"</code>
        and <code>ValidationMessage</code> shows the error, but nothing links the two: a screen reader says "invalid
        entry" and never reads the message. The test submits the empty form first, then audits.
    </p>
    <ExampleCard Title="The template form" Defect="true" Example="BlazorRuleExamples.LegacySignupAudit" Sources="@(["LegacySignupForm.razor"])"
                 Hears="Email, edit text, invalid entry" HearsLabel="After a failed submit, the email field is announced as" Affects="@(["Screen reader users"])">
        <ChildContent><p>Submit the empty form to see the errors.</p></ChildContent>
        <Stage><LegacySignupForm /></Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
    <ExampleCard Title="Errors referenced by aria-describedby" Example="BlazorRuleExamples.SignupAudit" Sources="@(["SignupForm.razor", "FieldError.razor"])"
                 Hears="Email, edit text, required, invalid entry, Enter your email address." HearsLabel="After a failed submit, the email field is announced as">
        <ChildContent><p>The error text is referenced by the field, focus moves to the first invalid field, and success is announced.</p></ChildContent>
        <Stage><SignupForm /></Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="ids">
    <h2 id="ids">Hard-coded ids in reusable components</h2>
    <p>
        Rule <code>blazor-duplicate-id-referenced</code>. A component with <code>id="quantity"</code> works until it is
        rendered twice. Then both labels point at the first input, the second input has no label, and both hints describe
        the first input.
    </p>
    <ExampleCard Title="A cart that reuses a field with fixed ids" Defect="true" Example="BlazorRuleExamples.LegacyCartAudit" Sources="@(["LegacyCartLines.razor", "HardcodedQuantityField.razor"])"
                 Hears="edit text" HearsLabel="The second quantity field is announced as" Affects="@(["Screen reader users", "Voice control users"])">
        <ChildContent><p>Click the second "Quantity" label: focus goes to the first input.</p></ChildContent>
        <Stage><LegacyCartLines /></Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
    <ExampleCard Title="A field with generated ids" Example="BlazorRuleExamples.CartAudit" Sources="@(["CartLines.razor", "QuantityField.razor"])"
                 Hears="Quantity of Paper filters, edit text" HearsLabel="The second quantity field is announced as">
        <Stage><CartLines /></Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="radios">
    <h2 id="radios">Radio buttons without a group</h2>
    <p>
        Rule <code>blazor-radio-group-ungrouped</code>. The question above a set of radio buttons is often a styled
        paragraph. Sighted users see it; screen reader users who tab into the group hear only "Express (next day), radio
        button, 2 of 3". A <code>fieldset</code> with a <code>legend</code> names the group.
    </p>
    <ExampleCard Title="A styled paragraph as the group label" Defect="true" Example="BlazorRuleExamples.UngroupedRadiosAudit" Sources="@(["UngroupedShippingOptions.razor"])"
                 Hears="Standard (3 to 5 days), radio button, checked, 1 of 3" HearsLabel="Tabbing into the options, a screen reader announces" Affects="@(["Screen reader users"])">
        <Stage><UngroupedShippingOptions /></Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
    <ExampleCard Title="A fieldset with a legend" Example="BlazorRuleExamples.GroupedRadiosAudit" Sources="@(["ShippingOptions.razor"])"
                 Hears="Delivery speed, grouping, Standard (3 to 5 days), radio button, checked, 1 of 3" HearsLabel="Tabbing into the options, a screen reader announces">
        <Stage><ShippingOptions /></Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
</section>

<p>Every toolkit rule, with its WCAG criteria and where it runs, is in <a href="rules">the rule catalog</a>.</p>
